<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 Skeletal Notes                                                -->
<!-- Section: Parametrization of Curves                                    -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Skeletal (fill-in) version of the section of the same name in the     -->
<!-- completed MATH 14 notes.  Definitions, the numbered results that are  -->
<!-- cited later, and problem statements are kept; every derivation and    -->
<!-- solution is replaced by a blank workspace box preceded by a one-line  -->
<!-- instruction.  File names and xml:ids are unchanged, so the            -->
<!-- assignments and review problems cross-reference this section exactly  -->
<!-- as before.                                                            -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Figures: the pictures that introduce an idea (the general trajectory, -->
<!-- the unit circle with its parameter marked, the no-collision picture,  -->
<!-- and the three space curves, which nobody could draw by hand) are kept -->
<!-- in full.  The plots that answer an exercise (the parabola from the    -->
<!-- table, the segment, the ellipse, the shifted circle, the collision    -->
<!-- picture) become empty grids to draw on, and the table of points has   -->
<!-- its x and y columns left blank.                                       -->
<!--                                                                       -->
<!-- Plane curves are drawn with PreFigure (annotated for screen readers); -->
<!-- space curves with TikZ/pgfplots, which needs the tikz + pgfplots      -->
<!-- preamble already present in main.ptx.                                 -->

<section xml:id="sec-parametrization">
  <title>Parametrization of Curves</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Describe the motion of a point in the plane with a pair of
          parametric equations <m>x=f(t)</m> and <m>y=g(t)</m>, and
          understand the parameter <m>t</m> as recording <em>when</em> the
          point visits each place on the curve.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Sketch a parametric curve from a table of values, and indicate the
          direction of increasing <m>t</m> in which the curve is traced.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Find parametric equations for common curves, such as lines,
          circles, and ellipses, and eliminate the parameter to recover a
          Cartesian equation when possible.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Distinguish the <term>intersection points</term> of two parametric
          curves from their <term>collision points</term>, where two moving
          objects reach the same place at the same time.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Write a space curve as a vector function
          <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>, and parametrize the curve in which two
          surfaces meet.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Imagine that you would like to describe the motion of an object whose
      motion is confined to a plane. If you know how its Cartesian
      coordinates, <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, change as time passes, you will be
      able to draw a curve that represents the trajectory of its motion.
      Mathematically, we will need two equations to demonstrate how the
      <m>x</m><ndash/> and <m>y</m>-coordinates evolve in time <m>t</m>,
      which are known as the parametric equations. This section reviews that
      language and then extends it to curves in space, where it becomes the
      main tool for everything that follows.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>These are skeletal notes: the definitions, theorems, and problem
      statements are given, and blank boxes are left wherever a derivation, a
      solution, or a sketch belongs, so that you can fill them in by hand.</em>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-parametric-equations">
    <title>Parametric Equations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-parametric-equations">
      <title>Parametric Equations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are given as functions of a third
          variable <m>t</m>, called a <term>parameter</term>, by

        <md>
          <mrow xml:id="eq-parametric-equations" number="yes">
            x = f(t), \qquad y = g(t),
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          then the points <m>(x,y) = \left(f(t),\,g(t)\right)</m> trace out a
          curve in the plane, called a <term>parametric curve</term>. The
          equations <m>x=f(t)</m> and <m>y=g(t)</m> are the
          <term>parametric equations</term> of the curve.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      As <m>t</m> runs over its interval, the point
      <m>\left(f(t),g(t)\right)</m> moves, and the trail it leaves behind is
      the curve. The parameter therefore carries more information than the
      picture does: it also records <em>when</em> the moving point is at each
      place, as in <xref ref="fig-trajectory"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-trajectory">
      <caption>
        The trajectory of a moving point whose coordinates are given by the
        parametric equations <m>x=f(t)</m>, <m>y=g(t)</m>. At the instant
        <m>t_0</m> the point is at
        <m>\left(f(t_0),g(t_0)\right)</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-trajectory" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
          A curved trajectory in the plane with a marked point and dashed
          lines dropping to the two coordinate axes.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A smooth curve rises and falls across the first quadrant from a
            starting point A, reached at time t equals a, to an ending point
            B, reached at time t equals b. A single point on the curve is
            marked; dashed gray segments run from it straight down to the x
            axis, where the value is labeled f of t nought, and straight left
            to the y axis, where the value is labeled g of t nought. An arrow
            on the curve shows the direction of increasing t.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="trajectory">
          <diagram dimensions="(420,320)" margins="5">
            <definition>c(t) = (t, 2 + sin(t))</definition>
            <definition>t0 = 2</definition>
            <definition>P = c(t0)</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-0.7,-0.6,5.9,4.2)">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

              <group at="curve">
                <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0,5)" stroke="blue"
                                  thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                  arrow-location="3.4"/>
                <label anchor="c(4.3)" alignment="se" offset="(6,-6)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>C</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="ends">
                <point p="c(0)" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                <label anchor="c(0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,4)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>A,\ t=a</m>
                </label>
                <point p="c(5)" size="4" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                <label anchor="c(5)" alignment="se" offset="(4,-4)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>B,\ t=b</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="readout">
                <line endpoints="(P, (t0,0))" stroke="gray" thickness="2"
                      dash="4 3"/>
                <line endpoints="(P, (0,2+sin(t0)))" stroke="gray"
                      thickness="2" dash="4 3"/>
                <point p="P" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="P" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>\left(f(t_0),g(t_0)\right)</m>
                </label>
                <label anchor="(t0,0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-8)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>f(t_0)</m>
                </label>
                <label anchor="(0,2+sin(t0))" alignment="w" offset="(-8,0)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>g(t_0)</m>
                </label>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="The trajectory of a point moving in the plane,
                                with its position at the instant t nought read
                                off on the two coordinate axes">
                <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                <annotation ref="curve"
                            text="The trajectory C, traced in the direction of
                                  increasing t"
                            sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="ends"
                            text="The starting point A at time t equals a and
                                  the ending point B at time t equals b"/>
                <annotation ref="readout"
                            text="The position of the point at the instant t
                                  nought, with dashed segments to the axes
                                  showing its coordinates f of t nought and g
                                  of t nought"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      As an example, consider <m>x = t</m> and <m>y = t^2</m>. Fill in
      <xref ref="table-parabola-points"/>, plot the seven points on the grid
      below, and join them. (Then eliminate the parameter to name the curve,
      and mark on your sketch the direction in which it is traced as
      <m>t</m> increases.)
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-parabola-points">
      <title>Points on the curve <m>x=t</m>, <m>y=t^2</m></title>
      <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="major">
          <cell><m>t</m></cell>
          <cell><m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>y</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell><m>-3</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>2</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
        <row><cell><m>3</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
      <image>
        <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.52, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-4,-1) grid (4,10);
\draw[->, thick] (-4,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,10.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12) node[below=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9} \draw (-0.12,\y) -- (0.12,\y) node[left=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
        <shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid running from negative four to four horizontally and negative one to ten vertically, for plotting the seven points from the table and joining them.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <image>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (7,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {Eliminating $t$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,3.2) {Name of the curve, and its direction:};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
        <shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for eliminating the parameter and one for naming the curve and the direction in which it is traced.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      As our second example, consider the equation of the circle centered at
      <m>(0,0)</m> with unit radius,

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-unit-circle" number="yes">
        x^2 + y^2 = 1.
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, we would like to parametrize <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> in terms of a
      single parameter <m>t</m>, as in <xref ref="eq-parametric-equations"/>,
      in such a way that by varying the value of <m>t</m> we can reconstruct
      the circle <xref ref="eq-unit-circle"/>. Find such a pair. (One
      trigonometric identity does the whole job; match it against
      <xref ref="eq-unit-circle"/> term by term. State the interval of
      <m>t</m> that traces the circle exactly once, and check your answer by
      substituting it back in.)
    </p>

    <sidebyside width="97%">
      <image>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {the identity, and the parametrization:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {interval of $t$, and the check:};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
        <shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the trigonometric identity and the resulting parametrization and one for the interval of the parameter and the verification.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <assemblage xml:id="summary-unit-circle-param">
      <title>The Unit Circle</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow xml:id="eq-unit-circle-param" number="yes">
            x = \cos(t), \qquad y = \sin(t), \qquad t \in [0, 2\pi).
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>
      The parametrization is illustrated in
      <xref ref="fig-unit-circle-param"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-unit-circle-param">
      <caption>
        Parametrizing the unit circle <m>x^2+y^2=1</m> by
        <m>x=\cos(t)</m>, <m>y=\sin(t)</m>, <m>t \in [0,2\pi)</m>. A generic
        point of the circle corresponds to the parameter value <m>t_0</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-unit-circle-param" width="60%">
        <shortdescription>
          The unit circle traced counterclockwise, with the four quarter
          points marked and one generic point at parameter value t nought.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            The unit circle on a coordinate grid, traced counterclockwise as
            indicated by an arrowhead. Four dots mark the points reached at t
            equals zero, at one comma zero; t equals pi over two, at zero
            comma one; t equals pi, at minus one comma zero; and t equals
            three pi over two, at zero comma minus one. A fifth dot part way
            around the first quadrant marks a generic point cosine t nought,
            sine t nought, joined to the origin by a segment that makes the
            angle t nought with the positive x axis.
          </p>
        </description>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="unit-circle-param">
          <diagram dimensions="(380,380)" margins="5">
            <definition>c(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
            <definition>t0 = 0.9</definition>
            <coordinates bbox="(-1.6,-1.6,1.6,1.6)" aspect-ratio="1">
              <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

              <group at="curve">
                <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0,2*pi)" stroke="blue"
                                  thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                  arrow-location="0.35"/>
              </group>

              <group at="quarters">
                <point p="c(0)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="c(0)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,4)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t=0</m>
                </label>
                <point p="c(pi/2)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="c(pi/2)" alignment="n" offset="(0,8)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t=\pi/2</m>
                </label>
                <point p="c(pi)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="c(pi)" alignment="w" offset="(-8,0)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t=\pi</m>
                </label>
                <point p="c(3*pi/2)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                <label anchor="c(3*pi/2)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-8)"
                       clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t=3\pi/2</m>
                </label>
              </group>

              <group at="generic">
                <line endpoints="((0,0), c(t0))" stroke="#CC79A7"
                      thickness="2"/>
                <point p="c(t0)" size="4.5" stroke="#CC79A7" fill="#CC79A7"/>
                <label anchor="c(t0)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)"
                       color="#CC79A7" clear-background="yes">
                  <m>\left(\cos t_0,\ \sin t_0\right)</m>
                </label>
                <label anchor="0.55*c(t0)" alignment="se" offset="(8,-6)"
                       color="#CC79A7" clear-background="yes">
                  <m>t_0</m>
                </label>
              </group>
            </coordinates>

            <annotations>
              <annotation ref="figure"
                          text="The unit circle parametrized by cosine t and
                                sine t, traced counterclockwise as t runs from
                                zero to two pi">
                <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                <annotation ref="curve"
                            text="The unit circle, traced counterclockwise"
                            sonify="yes"/>
                <annotation ref="quarters"
                            text="The four points reached at t equals zero, pi
                                  over two, pi, and three pi over two"/>
                <annotation ref="generic"
                            text="A generic point of the circle, at parameter
                                  value t nought, joined to the origin"/>
              </annotation>
            </annotations>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <exercise xml:id="check-circle-other-parametrization">
      <title>Question</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Can you think of another way to write a parametric form of the
          circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>?
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape] at (0.25,4.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank workspace for a second parametrization of the unit circle.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Nothing forces the point to start at <m>(1,0)</m>, to travel
          counterclockwise, or to go around exactly once.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-parametrizing-examples">
    <title>Some Examples of Parametrizing Curves</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-parametrizing-curves">
      <title>Parametrizing Curves</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the parametric equations corresponding to the following
          curves.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The line segment connecting the two points <m>(1,0)</m> and
              <m>(0,1)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The ellipse <m>\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The circle of radius <m>2</m> centered at <m>(2,0)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
          (In <alert>A</alert>, find the Cartesian equation of the line first
          and then take <m>x = t</m>; state the interval of <m>t</m> that
          gives the segment and no more of the line, and say where the point
          is at each end of the interval. Parts <alert>B</alert> and
          <alert>C</alert> are both adjustments of
          <xref ref="eq-unit-circle-param"/>: one rescales the two
          coordinates, the other shifts them. Verify each answer by
          substituting it into the Cartesian equation.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10) -- (15,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,5) -- (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,14.7) {A.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.7) {B.};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {C.};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three labelled rows, one for each of the three parametrizations together with its verification.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
          Now sketch the three curves on the grids below, marking the point
          reached at <m>t = 0</m> and the direction of increasing <m>t</m>
          on each.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="31% 31% 31%">
          <image>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-0.5,-0.5) grid (1.5,1.5);
\draw[->, thick] (-0.5,0) -- (1.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,1.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (1,-0.08) -- (1,0.08) node[below=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$1$};
\draw (-0.08,1) -- (0.08,1) node[left=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$1$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0.5,-0.75) {A.};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid for part A, for sketching the segment from zero comma one to one comma zero.</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.4, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-3.5,-4) grid (3.5,4);
\draw[->, thick] (-3.5,0) -- (3.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-4) -- (0,4.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (-0.12,\y) -- (0.12,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (0,-4.6) {B.};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid for part B, running from negative three to three horizontally and negative four to four vertically, for sketching the ellipse.</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.45, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-1,-2.5) grid (5,2.5);
\draw[->, thick] (-1,0) -- (5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.9) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} \draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw (-0.12,\y) -- (0.12,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\node[anchor=north, font=\small\itshape] at (2,-3.1) {C.};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid for part C, running from negative one to five horizontally and negative two and a half to two and a half vertically, for sketching the shifted circle.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-intersection-collision">
    <title>Intersection Points versus Collision Points</title>

    <p>
      A parametrization carries more information than the curve it traces: it
      also records <em>when</em> the moving point visits each place on the
      curve. Because of this, there are two different questions we can ask
      about two parametric curves

    <md>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{r}_1(t) \amp = \left\langle f_1(t),\, g_1(t)\right\rangle,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{r}_2(t) \amp = \left\langle f_2(t),\, g_2(t)\right\rangle,
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      thought of as the trajectories of two particles.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-intersection-collision">
      <title>Intersection Points and Collision Points</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A point <m>P</m> is an <term>intersection point</term> of the two
          curves if <m>P</m> lies on both curves; that is, if there are
          parameter values <m>t</m> and <m>s</m>, not necessarily equal, with

        <md>
          \mathbf{r}_1(t) = \overrightarrow{OP} = \mathbf{r}_2(s).
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          A point <m>P</m> is a <term>collision point</term> of the two
          particles if both particles are at <m>P</m> at the same time; that
          is, if there is a single value of <m>t</m> with

        <md>
          \mathbf{r}_1(t) = \mathbf{r}_2(t) = \overrightarrow{OP}.
        </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      An intersection point is a statement about the two <em>paths</em>: they
      cross, like two roads on a map. A collision point is a statement about
      the two <em>motions</em>: the cars are at the crossroads at the same
      instant. Every collision point is automatically an intersection point,
      but, as the next two examples show, an intersection point need not be a
      collision point.
    </p>

    <p>
      Say how the two definitions differ when you set up the equations, and
      how they are related. (One of the two searches for a <em>pair</em> of
      parameter values and the other for a <em>single</em> one; say which,
      and what that means for the names you give the parameters. Then decide
      whether every collision point is an intersection point, and whether
      the converse holds.)
    </p>

    <sidebyside width="97%">
      <image>
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {to find intersection points, solve:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {to find collision points, solve:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {which implies which:};
\end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
        <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the equations that give intersection points, for the equations that give collision points, and for the implication between the two.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </sidebyside>

    <p>
      In the next two examples the two paths are always the same: the
      parabola <m>y = x^2</m> and the line <m>y = x+2</m>, which meet at the
      two points

    <men xml:id="eq-parabola-line-intersections">
      (-1,1) \qquad \text{and} \qquad (2,4).
    </men>
    </p>

    <p>
      Only the schedule of the second particle will change.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-paths-cross-no-collision">
      <title>The paths cross, but the particles never meet</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two particles move in the plane with position vectors

        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_1(t) \amp = \left\langle t,\; t^2\right\rangle,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_2(t) \amp = \left\langle -t,\; 2-t\right\rangle,
                                   \qquad t \in \R .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Find all intersection points of their paths, and all collision
          points of the particles.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
          (Eliminate the parameter from each to confirm that the paths really
          are the parabola and the line named in
          <xref ref="eq-parabola-line-intersections"/>. Then set up the
          collision equations: both coordinates must agree for the
          <em>same</em> <m>t</m>, and here one of them pins <m>t</m> down
          immediately. Finish by finding, for each of the two crossings, the
          time at which each particle passes through it <mdash/> that is
          what shows how they miss. Then compare with
          <xref ref="fig-intersection-no-collision"/>.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {the two paths:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {collision equations:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {when each particle reaches each crossing:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the two paths after eliminating the parameter, for the collision equations, and for the times at which each particle reaches each crossing.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </example>
        <figure xml:id="fig-intersection-no-collision">
          <caption>
            The parabola <m>C_1</m> and the line <m>C_2</m> intersect at
            <m>(-1,1)</m> and <m>(2,4)</m>. At each of these points the two
            particles arrive at different times, so neither point is a
            collision point.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-intersection-no-collision" width="75%">
            <shortdescription>
              A parabola and a line crossing at two points, each labeled with
              the two different times at which the particles pass through it.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                The parabola y equals x squared and the line y equals x plus
                two drawn on the same coordinate grid, crossing at the points
                minus one comma one and two comma four. Each crossing is
                marked with a dot and labeled with two different parameter
                values: at minus one comma one the first particle arrives at t
                equals minus one and the second at t equals one, and at two
                comma four the first arrives at t equals two and the second at
                t equals minus two.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="intersection-no-collision">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                <definition>p(t) = (t, t^2)</definition>
                <definition>l(t) = (-t, 2-t)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-1.8,5.6,7.2)">
                  <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

                  <group at="parabola">
                    <!-- the arrow sits well up the right branch: at
                         t=2 it would land under the crossing point -->
                    <parametric-curve function="p" domain="(-2.4,2.6)"
                                      stroke="blue" thickness="3"
                                      arrows="1" arrow-location="2.45"/>
                    <label anchor="p(-2.3)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,4)"
                           color="blue">
                      <m>C_1</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="line">
                    <parametric-curve function="l" domain="(-3.4,3.1)"
                                      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"
                                      arrows="1" arrow-location="2"/>
                    <label anchor="l(-2.9)" alignment="se" offset="(8,-2)"
                           color="#009E73">
                      <m>C_2</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <!-- Each crossing is annotated in the empty wedge between
                       the two curves, with a thin leader back to the point,
                       so that no label sits on a curve or runs off the
                       edge of the diagram. -->
                  <group at="crossings">
                    <point p="(-1,1)" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <line endpoints="((-1.15,1.05), (-2.2,1.5))"
                          stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
                    <label anchor="(-3.1,3.1)" alignment="c">
                      <m>(-1,1)</m>
                    </label>
                    <label anchor="(-3.1,2.3)" alignment="c">
                      <m>C_1: t=-1</m>
                    </label>
                    <label anchor="(-3.1,1.5)" alignment="c">
                      <m>C_2: t=1</m>
                    </label>
                    <point p="(2,4)" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <line endpoints="((2.15,3.95), (3.05,3.7))"
                          stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
                    <label anchor="(3.85,3.7)" alignment="c">
                      <m>(2,4)</m>
                    </label>
                    <label anchor="(3.85,2.9)" alignment="c">
                      <m>C_1: t=2</m>
                    </label>
                    <label anchor="(3.85,2.1)" alignment="c">
                      <m>C_2: t=-2</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="A parabola and a line crossing at two
                                    points that the two particles reach at
                                    different times, so there is no collision">
                    <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                    <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                    <annotation ref="parabola"
                                text="The path of the first particle, the
                                      parabola y equals x squared"
                                sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="line"
                                text="The path of the second particle, the
                                      line y equals x plus two"
                                sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="crossings"
                                text="The two intersection points, each
                                      labeled with the two different times at
                                      which the particles pass through it"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-same-paths-collision">
      <title>The same paths, a different schedule<mdash/>now they collide</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keep the first particle, but re-schedule the second one:

        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_1(t) \amp = \left\langle t,\; t^2\right\rangle,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_2(t) \amp = \left\langle 2t-2,\; 2t\right\rangle,
                                   \qquad t \in \R .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Find the intersection points of the paths and the collision points
          of the particles.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
          (Eliminate the parameter again and compare with the previous
          example: the paths should be unchanged, so the intersection points
          are too. The collision equations, however, now have a solution;
          find it, and remember to check that the value of <m>t</m> from the
          first equation also satisfies the second. Then say what happens at
          the <em>other</em> crossing, and mark the collision on the grid.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.7) -- (7,6.7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.4) -- (7,3.4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,9.7) {the paths:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.4) {collision equations:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,3.1) {the other crossing:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the paths, for the collision computation, and for what happens at the other crossing.</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-4,-2) grid (4,6);
\draw[->, thick] (-4,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12) node[below=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} \draw (-0.12,\y) -- (0.12,\y) node[left=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid running from negative four to four horizontally and negative two to six vertically, for drawing the parabola and the line and marking the collision point.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="remark-collision-vs-intersection">
      <title>Every collision is an intersection, but not conversely</title>
      <p>
        If two particles collide at <m>P</m>, then certainly <m>P</m> lies on
        both paths, so every collision point is an intersection point. The
        converse fails: the paths may cross at a point that the two particles
        visit at different times. Changing the parametrization of a curve
        does not move the curve, but it can create or destroy collisions.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <exercise xml:id="check-collision-both-points">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keep <m>\mathbf{r}_1(t) = \left\langle t,t^2\right\rangle</m>. Find a parametrization
          <m>\mathbf{r}_2</m> of the line <m>y = x+2</m> for which the two
          particles collide at <em>both</em> intersection points.
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$\mathbf{r}_2(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {the collision equations it produces:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the new parametrization of the line and one for the collision equations it produces.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Try to arrange that the second particle has the same
          <m>x</m>-coordinate as the first one at every time <m>t</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-curves-in-space">
    <title>Curves in Space and Vector Functions</title>

    <p>
      Assume that we are interested in describing the path of an object in
      space. To this aim, we can specify its location at each instant of
      time; that is, we can write its coordinates in <m>\R^3</m> as

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-space-parametric" number="yes">
        x = f(t), \qquad y = g(t), \qquad z = h(t).
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Here <m>t</m> is the time parameter, which varies from some initial
      time <m>t_i</m> to some final time <m>t_f</m>, that is,
      <m>t \in [t_i, t_f]</m>. Nothing has changed except the bookkeeping:
      where a plane curve needed the two equations
      <xref ref="eq-parametric-equations"/>, a space curve needs three.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is a second, more compact way to look at
      <xref ref="eq-space-parametric"/>, and it is the point of view we will
      use for the rest of the course. Instead of three separate coordinate
      equations, collect the coordinates into a single
      <term>vector function</term> <mdash/> a function whose input is a real
      number and whose output is a vector:

    <md>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{r}(t) \amp = \left\langle f(t),\ g(t),\ h(t)
                             \right\rangle
      </mrow>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-vector-function" number="yes">
        \amp = f(t)\,\mathbf{i} + g(t)\,\mathbf{j} + h(t)\,\mathbf{k},
               \qquad t \in [t_i,t_f].
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Think of <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> as the <term>position vector</term> of
      the moving object: an arrow drawn from the origin to the object's
      location at time <m>t</m>. As <m>t</m> increases, the arrow swings
      around and changes length, and its <em>tip</em> sweeps out the curve.
      So a parametrization can be read in two equivalent ways.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Coordinatewise.</term> Three ordinary scalar functions
          <m>f</m>, <m>g</m>, <m>h</m> of one variable, one for each
          coordinate, as in <xref ref="eq-space-parametric"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Vectorially.</term> One vector-valued function
          <m>\mathbf{r}</m> of one variable, as in
          <xref ref="eq-vector-function"/>. The curve is the set of tips of
          the vectors <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The two descriptions carry exactly the same information, but the vector
      form is the more useful one: it lets us differentiate and integrate a
      whole motion at once, and <m>\mathbf{r}'(t)</m> will turn out to be the
      velocity of the moving object. The same device works in the plane,
      where <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle f(t), g(t)\right\rangle</m>; the
      position vectors <m>\mathbf{r}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{r}_2</m> of
      <xref ref="ex-paths-cross-no-collision"/> were already written this
      way.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-helix-space-curve">
      <title>A helix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the object whose coordinates are parametrized by

        <md>
          <mrow>
            x \amp = \cos(2t), \qquad y = \sin(2t),
          </mrow>
          <mrow xml:id="eq-helix-param-space" number="yes">
            z \amp = t, \qquad t \in [0,\pi].
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Write the motion as a vector function and describe the path.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
          (Collect the three coordinates of
          <xref ref="eq-helix-param-space"/> into one vector function. Then
          describe the path: what equation do the first two coordinates
          satisfy, how many times does the shadow of the path on the
          <m>xy</m>-plane go around as <m>t</m> runs over <m>[0,\pi]</m>,
          and what does <m>z</m> do meanwhile? Name the curve, and give the
          points where the motion starts and ends. Then compare with
          <xref ref="fig-helix-space-curve"/>.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$\mathbf{r}(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {the shadow on the $xy$-plane, and how often it goes around:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {what $z$ does:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {the path, its start, and its end:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the vector function, for the shadow of the path on the x y plane, for the behaviour of the height, and for the name of the curve with its starting and ending points.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
        <figure xml:id="fig-helix-space-curve">
          <caption>
            One turn of the helix
            <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle \cos(2t), \sin(2t), t
            \right\rangle</m>, <m>0 \le t \le \pi</m>. The violet arrow is
            the position vector <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> at
            <m>t = 3\pi/8</m>; its tip rides along the curve as <m>t</m>
            increases. The dashed gray circle is the shadow of the path on
            the plane <m>z=0</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-helix-space-curve" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>
              One turn of a helix rising above the unit circle, with the
              position vector drawn from the origin to a point on the curve.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
                minus 1.3 to 1.3 and z from 0 to about 3.4. A thick blue curve
                spirals counterclockwise once around the vertical axis while
                climbing steadily, starting at a red point labeled (1,0,0) at
                time t equals zero on the floor of the box and ending at a red
                point labeled (1,0,pi) at time t equals pi, directly above the
                start. A dashed gray unit circle on the floor is the shadow of
                the path. A violet arrow labeled r of t runs from the origin
                to a point partway up the curve, illustrating the position
                vector whose tip traces the helix.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
              \begin{axis}[
                  width=10cm, view={115}{18},
                  xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                  xmin=-1.3, xmax=1.3, ymin=-1.3, ymax=1.3,
                  zmin=0, zmax=3.4,
                  grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
                ]
                % shadow of the path on the plane z=0
                \addplot3[gray!65, dashed, line width=1.3pt, domain=0:180,
                          samples=120, samples y=0]
                    ({cos(2*x)}, {sin(2*x)}, {0});
                % the helix itself
                \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:180,
                          samples=200, samples y=0]
                    ({cos(2*x)}, {sin(2*x)}, {pi*x/180});
                % the position vector r(t) at t = 3pi/8
                \addplot3[-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]}, violet, thick]
                    coordinates {(0,0,0) (-0.70711,0.70711,1.17810)};
                \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, violet, mark size=1.8pt]
                    coordinates {(-0.70711,0.70711,1.17810)};
                % start and end of the motion
                \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.8pt]
                    coordinates {(1,0,0) (1,0,3.14159)};
                \node[left=4pt, yshift=7pt] at (axis cs:1,0,0)
                    {\((1,0,0),\ t=0\)};
                \node[above right] at (axis cs:1,0,3.14159)
                    {\((1,0,\pi),\ t=\pi\)};
                \node[violet, above left=-2pt]
                    at (axis cs:-0.35355,0.35355,0.58905)
                    {\(\mathbf{r}(t)\)};
              \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      There are situations in which a curve in space arises as the
      intersection of two surfaces. In the next two examples we turn such a
      description into a vector function <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-cylinder-plane">
      <title>Where a plane cuts a cylinder</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a vector function that represents the curve of intersection of
          the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> and the plane
          <m>y + z = 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
          (The shadow of the curve on the <m>xy</m>-plane is the circle
          <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, whose parametrization you already know from
          <xref ref="eq-unit-circle-param"/>. Use the equation of the plane
          to write <m>z</m> in terms of <m>t</m>, assemble
          <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>, and state the range of <m>t</m>. As a check,
          confirm that <m>\mathbf{r}(0)</m>, <m>\mathbf{r}(\pi/2)</m>,
          <m>\mathbf{r}(\pi)</m>, and <m>\mathbf{r}(3\pi/2)</m> satisfy both
          equations, and compare with <xref ref="fig-cylinder-plane"/>.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$x(t)$ and $y(t)$, from the cylinder:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {$z(t)$, from the plane:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$\mathbf{r}(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {check at $t = 0,\ \pi/2,\ \pi,\ 3\pi/2$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the x and y coordinates from the cylinder, for z from the plane, for the assembled vector function with the range of t, and for the check at four parameter values.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cylinder-plane">
          <caption>
            The cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, the plane <m>y+z=2</m>, and the
            ellipse <m>C</m> in which they meet. The violet arrow is the
            position vector <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> of your parametrization at
            <m>t=\pi/4</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-cylinder-plane" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>
              A vertical cylinder cut by a slanted plane, with the ellipse
              where they meet drawn on the surfaces.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A three-dimensional plot showing a vertical circular cylinder
                of radius one standing on the xy-plane, and a slanted plane
                cutting through it. The two meet along a closed curve, an
                ellipse, drawn as a thick magenta loop and labeled C. Four
                points on the ellipse are marked, at (1,0,2), (0,1,1),
                (-1,0,2) and (0,-1,3). A violet arrow runs from the origin to
                a point on the ellipse, illustrating the position vector r of
                t.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
              \begin{axis}[
                  width=10.5cm, view={120}{16},
                  xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                  xmin=-1.9, xmax=1.9, ymin=-1.9, ymax=1.9,
                  zmin=0, zmax=3.6,
                  grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
                ]
                % the cylinder x^2 + y^2 = 1
                \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, opacity=0.30, draw=gray!45,
                          colormap={cylgray}{color=(gray!15) color=(gray!45)},
                          domain=0:360, y domain=0:3.2,
                          samples=48, samples y=2]
                    ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {y});
                % the plane y + z = 2
                \draw[blue!55, fill=blue!25, fill opacity=0.30]
                    (axis cs:-1.9,-1.6,3.6) -- (axis cs:1.9,-1.6,3.6)
                    -- (axis cs:1.9,1.9,0.1) -- (axis cs:-1.9,1.9,0.1)
                    -- cycle;
                % the curve of intersection
                \addplot3[magenta, very thick, domain=0:360,
                          samples=180, samples y=0]
                    ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {2-sin(x)});
                % the position vector r(pi/4)
                \addplot3[-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]}, violet, thick]
                    coordinates {(0,0,0) (0.70711,0.70711,1.29289)};
                \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, black, mark size=1.6pt]
                    coordinates {(1,0,2) (0,1,1) (-1,0,2) (0,-1,3)};
                \node[right=1pt] at (axis cs:1,0,2) {\((1,0,2)\)};
                \node[below=1pt] at (axis cs:0,1,1) {\((0,1,1)\)};
                \node[left=1pt] at (axis cs:-1,0,2) {\((-1,0,2)\)};
                \node[above=1pt] at (axis cs:0,-1,3) {\((0,-1,3)\)};
                \node[magenta, above left=-2pt]
                    at (axis cs:-0.70711,-0.70711,2.70711) {\(C\)};
                \node[violet, above left=-2pt]
                    at (axis cs:0.35355,0.35355,0.64645)
                    {\(\mathbf{r}(t)\)};
              \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-two-surfaces">
      <title>Where two curved surfaces meet</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Parametrize the intersection of the surfaces

        <md>
          z = \sqrt{1 + x^2 + y^2}
          \qquad \text{and} \qquad
          z = \sqrt{4 - 4x^2 - y^2} .
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
          (On the intersection the two expressions for <m>z</m> agree.
          Square both sides and simplify: <m>z</m> drops out, and what is
          left is the equation of the shadow of the curve in the
          <m>xy</m>-plane. Put it in the standard form of an ellipse and
          parametrize it as you did in
          <xref ref="ex-parametrizing-curves"/>; then recover <m>z(t)</m>
          from either surface and assemble <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>, with its
          range of <m>t</m>. Compare with <xref ref="fig-two-surfaces"/>.)
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {equate the two expressions for $z$ and square:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {the shadow, in standard form:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$x(t)$ and $y(t)$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$z(t)$, and $\mathbf{r}(t) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for equating and squaring the two expressions for z, for the equation of the shadow in standard form, for the parametrization of the shadow, and for z of t with the assembled vector function.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
        <figure xml:id="fig-two-surfaces">
          <caption>
            The upper sheet of <m>z = \sqrt{1+x^2+y^2}</m> (blue) and the
            dome <m>z = \sqrt{4-4x^2-y^2}</m> (red) meet along the closed
            curve drawn in black. Its shadow in the plane <m>z=0</m> is the
            dashed ellipse; compare it with the equation you found.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-two-surfaces" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
              A bowl-shaped surface and a dome overlapping, with the closed
              curve where they meet drawn on them.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A three-dimensional plot showing two surfaces. The first, in
                blue, is the upper sheet of a hyperboloid opening upward from
                its lowest point at height one above the origin. The second,
                in red, is a dome, the upper half of an ellipsoid, reaching
                height two above the origin. The two surfaces cross along a
                closed curve, drawn in black, which loops around the vertical
                axis at heights between about 1.26 and 1.58. A dashed ellipse
                on the floor of the box shows the shadow of that curve.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
              \begin{axis}[
                  width=10.5cm, view={120}{16},
                  xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                  xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2,
                  zmin=0, zmax=2.4,
                  grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
                  % the surfaces are named in a legend rather than on the plot
                  legend style={at={(1.02,1)}, anchor=north west,
                                draw=gray!45, fill=white, font=\small,
                                cells={anchor=west}, row sep=2pt},
                  legend cell align=left,
                ]
                % the dome z = sqrt(4 - 4x^2 - y^2)
                \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, opacity=0.50, draw=red!55,
                          colormap={domered}{color=(red!25) color=(red!55)},
                          domain=0:90, y domain=0:360,
                          samples=16, samples y=48]
                    ({sin(x)*cos(y)}, {2*sin(x)*sin(y)}, {2*cos(x)});
                % the upper sheet z = sqrt(1 + x^2 + y^2)
                \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, opacity=0.50, draw=blue!55,
                          colormap={sheetblue}{color=(blue!25) color=(blue!55)},
                          domain=0:1.5, y domain=0:360,
                          samples=14, samples y=48]
                    ({x*cos(y)}, {x*sin(y)}, {sqrt(1+x^2)});
                % shadow of the intersection curve
                \addplot3[gray!70, dashed, line width=2pt, domain=0:360,
                          samples=120, samples y=0]
                    ({0.774597*cos(x)}, {1.224745*sin(x)}, {0});
                % the curve of intersection
                \addplot3[black, very thick, domain=0:360,
                          samples=180, samples y=0]
                    ({0.774597*cos(x)}, {1.224745*sin(x)},
                     {sqrt(1+0.6*(cos(x))^2+1.5*(sin(x))^2)});
                % One entry per plot, in the order the plots were drawn:
                % dome, sheet, shadow, curve.  (An \addlegendentry binds to
                % the next plot without a label, so the order matters.)
                \legend{\(z=\sqrt{4-4x^2-y^2}\),
                        \(z=\sqrt{1+x^2+y^2}\),
                        shadow of the curve\, \(z=0\),
                        curve of intersection}
              \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

</section>
